<text top="134" left="103" width="690" height="22" font="1">Position Sensitivity and Lexical Competition in Synthetic Clause Retrieval</text>
<text top="161" left="309" width="277" height="22" font="1">with a Small Language Model</text>
<text top="241" left="374" width="134" height="15" font="2">Anonymous Authors</text>
<text top="239" left="508" width="6" height="10" font="3">1</text>
<text top="290" left="225" width="67" height="18" font="4">Abstract</text>
<text top="323" left="112" width="294" height="15" font="2">Where an answer appears and which com-</text>
<text top="341" left="113" width="291" height="15" font="2">peting text survives selection may govern</text>
<text top="359" left="113" width="292" height="15" font="2">clause retrieval more clearly than prompt</text>
<text top="377" left="113" width="294" height="15" font="2">length in a small language model. We evalu-</text>
<text top="395" left="113" width="294" height="15" font="2">ate the frozen Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct Q8 arti-</text>
<text top="412" left="113" width="294" height="15" font="2">fact on 180 deterministic synthetic contract-</text>
<text top="430" left="113" width="291" height="15" font="2">clause probes spanning mean prompt lengths</text>
<text top="448" left="113" width="291" height="15" font="2">of 1989, 3986, and 7992 tokens. Moving the</text>
<text top="466" left="113" width="291" height="15" font="2">answer clause from the start to the end raised</text>
<text top="484" left="113" width="292" height="15" font="2">accuracy by 58.3 points at the shortest tier</text>
<text top="502" left="113" width="246" height="15" font="2">and 50.0 points at the middle tier.</text>
<text top="502" left="376" width="31" height="15" font="2">Key-</text>
<text top="520" left="113" width="292" height="15" font="2">word pruning reduced accuracy from 40.0%</text>
<text top="538" left="113" width="291" height="15" font="2">to 6.7% because it retained a clause whose</text>
<text top="556" left="113" width="292" height="15" font="2">wording matched the question but whose</text>
<text top="574" left="113" width="294" height="15" font="2">code was wrong. A query-entity selector ex-</text>
<text top="592" left="113" width="292" height="15" font="2">cluded that lexical decoy and reached 100.0%</text>
<text top="610" left="113" width="291" height="15" font="2">accuracy on the same 30 cases. The matched</text>
<text top="628" left="113" width="291" height="15" font="2">length comparison remained inconclusive, so</text>
<text top="646" left="113" width="291" height="15" font="2">the practical finding is narrower: test a cheap</text>
<text top="664" left="113" width="291" height="15" font="2">selector against plausible lexical competitors</text>
<text top="681" left="113" width="291" height="15" font="2">before using it to shorten documents for this</text>
<text top="699" left="113" width="44" height="15" font="2">model.</text>
<text top="756" left="83" width="116" height="18" font="4">1. Introduction</text>
<text top="787" left="83" width="354" height="15" font="2">You paste a long contract into a chatbot and ask,</text>
<text top="805" left="81" width="356" height="15" font="2">“What code renews the vendor agreement?” The an-</text>
<text top="823" left="83" width="353" height="15" font="2">swer sits in one clause, but a similar buyer clause re-</text>
<text top="841" left="83" width="351" height="15" font="2">peats the words in your question and gives a different</text>
<text top="859" left="83" width="34" height="15" font="2">code.</text>
<text top="886" left="83" width="352" height="15" font="2">This ordinary lookup becomes less predictable in a</text>
<text top="904" left="83" width="353" height="15" font="2">long prompt. The model may fail because it must pro-</text>
<text top="922" left="83" width="351" height="15" font="2">cess a longer document, because the answer appears in</text>
<text top="940" left="83" width="351" height="15" font="2">a weak position, or because nearby text resembles the</text>
<text top="958" left="83" width="353" height="15" font="2">question. Each explanation points to a different diag-</text>
<text top="985" left="101" width="5" height="9" font="5">1</text>
<text top="987" left="108" width="326" height="13" font="6">Anonymous Institution, Anonymous City, Anonymous</text>
<text top="1002" left="83" width="353" height="13" font="6">Region, Anonymous Country. Correspondence to: Anony-</text>
<text top="1017" left="83" width="258" height="13" font="6">mous Author &lt;anon.email@domain.com&gt;.</text>
<text top="1047" left="83" width="353" height="13" font="6">Preliminary work. Under review by the International Con-</text>
<text top="1062" left="83" width="345" height="13" font="6">ference on Machine Learning (ICML). Do not distribute.</text>
<text top="293" left="461" width="351" height="15" font="2">nosis: compare matched prompt lengths, move the key</text>
<text top="311" left="461" width="351" height="15" font="2">clause, or inspect what a filter retains. Distinguishing</text>
<text top="329" left="461" width="351" height="15" font="2">among them requires an evaluation that holds scenario</text>
<text top="347" left="461" width="217" height="15" font="2">content fixed across comparisons.</text>
<text top="374" left="460" width="354" height="15" font="2">We study these choices in a synthetic, gradeable ver-</text>
<text top="392" left="461" width="353" height="15" font="2">sion of the contract situation. Each document con-</text>
<text top="409" left="461" width="353" height="15" font="2">tains an answer-bearing vendor clause and a look-</text>
<text top="427" left="461" width="351" height="15" font="2">alike clause for another company. The question uses</text>
<text top="445" left="461" width="351" height="15" font="2">the wording of the look-alike clause, while the answer</text>
<text top="463" left="461" width="354" height="15" font="2">clause expresses the same relation with different words.</text>
<text top="481" left="461" width="354" height="15" font="2">This construction reduces the value of literal matching,</text>
<text top="499" left="461" width="241" height="15" font="2">following the motivation of NoLiMa (</text>
<text top="499" left="702" width="108" height="15" font="7"><a href="main.html#4">Modarressi et al.</a></text>
<text top="499" left="810" width="4" height="15" font="2">,</text>
<text top="517" left="461" width="30" height="15" font="7"><a href="main.html#4">2025</a></text>
<text top="517" left="491" width="10" height="15" font="2">).</text>
<text top="544" left="460" width="352" height="15" font="2">We provide a paired evaluation of a frozen open model</text>
<text top="562" left="461" width="351" height="15" font="2">at laptop scale. Prior work established position and</text>
<text top="580" left="461" width="215" height="15" font="2">length failures in larger systems (</text>
<text top="580" left="676" width="58" height="15" font="7"><a href="main.html#4">Liu et al.</a></text>
<text top="580" left="734" width="4" height="15" font="2">,</text>
<text top="580" left="743" width="30" height="15" font="7"><a href="main.html#4">2023</a></text>
<text top="580" left="772" width="4" height="15" font="2">;</text>
<text top="580" left="781" width="32" height="15" font="7"><a href="main.html#4">Levy</a></text>
<text top="598" left="461" width="32" height="15" font="7"><a href="main.html#4">et al.</a></text>
<text top="598" left="493" width="4" height="15" font="2">,</text>
<text top="598" left="501" width="30" height="15" font="7"><a href="main.html#4">2024</a></text>
<text top="598" left="531" width="281" height="15" font="2">) and showed that irrelevant statements can</text>
<text top="616" left="461" width="122" height="15" font="2">disrupt reasoning <a href="main.html#4">(</a></text>
<text top="616" left="583" width="57" height="15" font="7"><a href="main.html#4">Shi et al.</a></text>
<text top="616" left="641" width="4" height="15" font="2"><a href="main.html#4">,</a></text>
<text top="616" left="649" width="30" height="15" font="7"><a href="main.html#4">2023</a></text>
<text top="616" left="679" width="136" height="15" font="2"><a href="main.html#4">). </a>Our main compar-</text>
<text top="634" left="461" width="351" height="15" font="2">ison moves the same answer clause while preserving</text>
<text top="652" left="461" width="354" height="15" font="2">the question, clause wording, codes, and filler order.</text>
<text top="669" left="460" width="352" height="15" font="2">We then compare a deterministic keyword filter with a</text>
<text top="687" left="461" width="351" height="15" font="2">query-entity selector that keeps sentences naming the</text>
<text top="705" left="461" width="351" height="15" font="2">company in the question. This selector comparison</text>
<text top="723" left="461" width="351" height="15" font="2">tests whether shortening helps when the filter does or</text>
<text top="741" left="461" width="250" height="15" font="2">does not retain the lexical competitor.</text>
<text top="768" left="461" width="243" height="15" font="2">The experiment asks three questions:</text>
<text top="805" left="472" width="340" height="15" font="2">1. How does moving the same answer clause among</text>
<text top="823" left="491" width="289" height="15" font="2">the start, middle, and end change accuracy?</text>
<text top="852" left="472" width="340" height="15" font="2">2. How much accuracy do keyword overlap and</text>
<text top="869" left="491" width="323" height="15" font="2">query-entity selection preserve on the same cases,</text>
<text top="887" left="491" width="221" height="15" font="2">and which clauses do they retain?</text>
<text top="916" left="472" width="341" height="15" font="2">3. As mean prompt length grows from 1989 to 7992</text>
<text top="934" left="491" width="321" height="15" font="2">tokens, how much does accuracy change on shared</text>
